  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "995fb729-c1b2-4c93-bee2-7cdbeded70bb",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        64,
        528
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 480,
        "height": 896,
        "content": "## Insurance Claim Auto-Router (Jev)\n\n### How it works\n\nThis workflow receives a submitted insurance claim, normalizes key fields, checks prior claim history, and uses a Jev router to classify the claim into escalation, review, senior review, or auto-processing paths. Claims requiring human review get an AI-generated briefing, are sent to a reviewer in Slack, and the reviewer\u2019s approval or rejection is logged. Approved and auto-process claims flow into an AI payout calculator, then a customer email is drafted and sent, while claim and decision records are written to Google Sheets.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- Configure the webhook endpoint used by the Claim Submitted trigger and ensure incoming payloads contain claimant, amount, claim_text, and claimant_email fields.\n- Connect Google Sheets credentials and set the correct spreadsheets/ranges for claim history lookup, initial or final decision logging, and auto-process claim logging.\n- Configure the Jev Router rules or model settings so it outputs the expected route values consumed by Route Decision.\n- Add DeepSeek credentials for all AI model subnodes used by the reviewer briefing, payout calculator agent, and customer email drafting chain.\n- Configure Slack credentials, reviewer channel/user, and the expected response format for the reviewer approval decision.\n- Configure Gmail credentials and sender details for customer notification emails.\n\n### Customization\n\nAdjust routing criteria, status labels, reviewer prompts, payout calculation instructions, Slack reviewer destinations, Google Sheet schemas, and the customer email template to match the insurer\u2019s policies."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
